<h1 class="title">WearTrail: A Scalable Multi-Station Industrial RFID and Zero-Knowledge Blockchain Ledger Architecture for Garment Supply Chain Traceability</h1>

<p class="authors">Supervisor: [Supervisor Name], Student 1: [Student 1 Name], Student 2: [Student 2 Name], Student 3: [Student 3 Name]</p>

<div class="abstract-box">
  <b>Abstract:</b> Global textile and garment supply chains face acute scrutiny regarding material authenticity, ethical labor compliance, and environmental provenance. Traditional manufacturing environments still depend heavily on manual barcode scans, localized ERP databases, or paper traveler sheets—mechanisms vulnerable to recording delays, physical tampering, and data silos. Furthermore, emerging regulatory frameworks, such as the European Union Digital Product Passport (DPP), necessitate verifiable, end-to-end transparency without compromising proprietary factory operations or worker privacy. This paper presents <b>WearTrail</b>, an integrated industrial Internet of Things (IoT) and blockchain system engineered for modern apparel production. WearTrail couples a nine-station physical Radio Frequency Identification (RFID) workflow with an EVM-compatible zero-knowledge commitment store. We introduce: (i) an automated edge hardware ingestion pipeline that disambiguates fabric rolls, individual garments, and shipping containers in sub-120ms cycles; (ii) a deterministic quality gate mechanism that dynamically blocks packing, warehousing, and dispatch upon active defect holds; (iii) an aggregate carton dispatch engine that executes atomic mass transitions across linked child garments via single-point container scans; and (iv) an asynchronous outbox worker utilizing salted cryptographic commitments (<font face="Courier New">sha256_nonce_v1</font>) to commit tamper-proof operational milestones to a distributed ledger while keeping proprietary operator logs strictly confidential. An empirical evaluation conducted across 16 comprehensive integration test suites and real-time hardware testbeds confirms that the proposed architecture achieves 100% transactional consistency across split cutting and aggregate carton workflows, zero defect leakage, and linear ingestion scalability under heavy industrial line loads.
</div>

<p class="keywords"><b>Keywords:</b> Blockchain, Supply Chain Traceability, Radio Frequency Identification (RFID), Digital Product Passport (DPP), Zero-Knowledge Commitments, Industrial IoT, Quality Gating, Smart Contracts, Garment Manufacturing.</p>

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>Over the years following the digital transformation of modern manufacturing, supply chain transparency has transitioned from a competitive advantage into a mandatory compliance requirement. The textile and ready-made garment (RMG) sector operates across highly fragmented, multi-tiered networks. Raw materials (such as organic cotton yarns and dyed fabric bolts) pass through yarn receiving, issue stores, cutting tables, stitching lines, finishing bays, quality checkpoints, packing areas, and intermediate warehouses before reaching outbound logistics carriers.</p>

<p>Despite technological advances in cloud computing, the physical shop floor remains plagued by structural vulnerabilities: (1) manual traveler tags and paper logs frequently become detached from garment bundles, creating gaps in material lineage; (2) barcode-based optical scanning requires direct line-of-sight alignment by human operators, causing throughput bottlenecks at fast-moving workstations; (3) siloed enterprise databases provide zero mathematical guarantee against retroactive record manipulation, internal record deletion, or false certification claims; and (4) naive blockchain implementations suffer from high gas consumption, low transaction throughput, and critical privacy leaks—exposing proprietary production costs, worker identities, and commercial yields directly to public ledgers.</p>

<p>To resolve this conflict between tamper-proof verifiability and enterprise confidentiality, this paper introduces <b>WearTrail</b>: an industrial garment traceability platform that unifies high-frequency RFID workstation automation with an asynchronous, zero-knowledge blockchain commitment architecture. In WearTrail, physical assets are bound to unique RFID transponders at nine discrete manufacturing stations. At the shop-floor edge, an intelligent hardware ingestion engine debounces rapid reads, resolves subject identity (fabric lot, individual garment, or container package), and enforces strict operational prerequisites. Off-chain state transitions are managed within a high-performance relational database (<font face="Courier New">supplyease</font> schema), while milestone validity is preserved through salted cryptographic hash commitments enqueued to an EVM smart contract (<font face="Courier New">WearTrailCommitmentStore.sol</font>). Concurrently, an anonymized Digital Product Passport (DPP) endpoint (<font face="Courier New">/p/:publicId</font>) allows retail shoppers and auditors to verify garment authenticity and material origins without requiring specialized software, cryptocurrency wallets, or authentication tokens.</p>

<p>The paper is organized as follows: Section 2 provides the background and related work of blockchain scalability and industrial RFID tracking. Section 3 provides the proposed WearTrail model, system architecture, operational workflow, and mathematical commitment model. Section 4 provides experimentation, testbed configurations, and benchmarking setups. Section 5 also includes specifications of various scenarios and an evaluation of observed results. Section 6 shows formal security and privacy analysis, and finally Section 7 focuses on conclusion and future work.</p>

<h2>2. Background & Related Work</h2>
<p>The initial application of blockchain technology, popularized by Bitcoin and Ethereum, established decentralized consensus without trusted intermediaries. However, standard public blockchains encounter severe scalability limits when applied to high-throughput industrial supply chains. Public Proof-of-Work (PoW) and Proof-of-Stake (PoS) networks exhibit block confirmation latencies ranging from seconds to minutes, which are incompatible with assembly line operations requiring sub-second physical tag feedback.</p>

<p>In response, permissioned and enterprise ledger frameworks—such as Hyperledger Fabric, MultiChain, and private EVM subnets—were developed to provide higher throughput and deterministic block finality. Nevertheless, persisting raw supply chain sensor feeds directly on-chain creates unsustainable state bloat and introduces severe commercial confidentiality risks. If an apparel factory records operator identities, detailed seam defect notes, and exact roll consumption yields directly onto a public or consortium ledger, competing manufacturers can reverse-engineer internal operational efficiency, piece-rate wages, and supplier pricing agreements.</p>

