<p class="caption">Fig. 1. Sample WearTrail Cryptographic Ledger Commitment and Public Payload Structure</p>

<p>To reconcile verification with privacy, modern cryptographic systems employ zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) and off-chain salted commitments. Under this model (depicted in Fig. 1), the full operational dataset resides in a secured off-chain relational environment, while only a fixed-length cryptographic digest is anchored into the smart contract state. This guarantees that any unauthorized modification of internal history immediately invalidates the published hash commitment without exposing private operational attributes.</p>

<h2>3. Proposed Model</h2>

<h3>3.1 Architectural Decomposition</h3>
<p>WearTrail employs a five-layer cyber-physical architecture designed for industrial garment execution: (1) Physical RFID Hardware Layer consisting of fixed antenna arrays and desktop readers; (2) Edge Ingestion and Deduplication Engine that debounces tag reads within a 1,500ms sliding window and auto-resolves subject entities; (3) Relational Enterprise Persistence Layer managing fifty normalized tables under PostgreSQL schema <font face="Courier New">supplyease</font>; (4) Asynchronous Zero-Knowledge Ledger Outbox that publishes salted root hashes to <font face="Courier New">WearTrailCommitmentStore.sol</font>; and (5) Public Digital Product Passport (DPP) Projection serving consumer verification at <font face="Courier New">/p/:publicId</font>.</p>

<pre class="code-block">
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              Physical Shop Floor Layer                                  |
|  [S1: Receiving] -> [S2: Issue] -> [S3: Cutting] -> [S4: Stitching] -> [S5: Finishing]  |
|  [S6: Final QC]  -> [S7: Packing] -> [S8: Warehouse] -> [S9: Outbound Dispatch Gate]   |
+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
                                             | RFID Hardware Scans (UID, ReaderID, UTC)
                                             v
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         Edge Ingestion & Deduplication Bridge                           |
|  • 1500ms Sliding Window Debounce   • Automatic Subject Resolution (Roll/Garment/Box)   |
+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
                                             | Validated Station Ingestion Event
                                             v
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                       Relational Enterprise Database (PostgreSQL)                       |
|  • Material Stock Movements   • Serialization Lineage   • Quality Holds & Rework Audit  |
|  • Packages & Aggregation     • Carrier Manifests       • Immutable Trace Events        |
+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+
                      |
                      +---------------------------------------+
                      | Internal Trace Event                  | Public State Projection
                      v                                       v
+------------------------------------------+    +-----------------------------------------+
|     Asynchronous Ledger Outbox Engine    |    |   Consumer Digital Product Passport     |
|  • Salted Nonce Generator (sha256_v1)    |    |   • Route: /p/:publicId                 |
|  • Batching Outbox Queue Worker          |    |   • Mathematical Proof Verification     |
+---------------------+--------------------+    |   • Zero Login / Zero Wallet Overhead   |
                      | Batch Commitments       +-----------------------------------------+
                      v
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                        EVM Blockchain Commitment Store Contract                         |
|                WearTrailCommitmentStore.sol (Immutable Root Hashes)                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
</pre>
<p class="caption">Fig. 2. Proposed WearTrail Multi-Station RFID and Blockchain-Anchored Architecture</p>

<h3>3.2 Nine Physical Workstation Workflow</h3>
<p>The manufacturing journey is governed by nine physical workstations:</p>
<p><b>Station 1 (Fabric Receiving):</b> Ingests raw fabric bolts from textile mills. The operator binds a master RFID card to the roll, registering supplier origin, yarn fiber composition, mill lot code, and physical weight.</p>
<p><b>Station 2 (Fabric Issue):</b> Operates within raw storage bays. When a production order is scheduled, the roll is scanned and allocated, creating an authorized stock movement record that deducts usable length.</p>
<p><b>Station 3 (Cutting & Serialization):</b> Performs first-scan validation on the cutting table. Upon cutting completion, the workstation serializes individual child garment codes (e.g. SHIRT-001 through SHIRT-050) and binds each to a physical garment RFID tag, establishing unbroken raw-to-garment lineage.</p>
<p><b>Station 4 (Stitching Assembly):</b> Tracks component assembly along sewing lines, logging cycle times and operator IDs.</p>
<p><b>Station 5 (Finishing & Pressing):</b> Manages thread trimming, chemical wash baths, pressing, and buttoning.</p>
<p><b>Station 6 (Final Quality Inspection):</b> Executes decision-first multi-point checks. Passed items advance to inspected. Defective items trigger an immediate garment hold, generating a rework case token routed to a repair technician; irreparable items are discarded with permanent audit notes.</p>
<p><b>Station 7 (Packing & Carton Aggregation):</b> Operators fold and bag garments into shipping boxes. The system verifies that every child item is hold-free, binds them to a master container RFID tag, and seals the carton.</p>
<p><b>Station 8 (Finished Goods Warehouse):</b> Scans cartons into designated warehouse storage racks and pallet bins, maintaining inventory balance.</p>
<p><b>Station 9 (Dispatch & Outbound Gate):</b> Long-range dock antennas scan master cartons during truck loading. Single-scan detection automatically triggers atomic mass transitions across all child garments directly to dispatched, while rejecting any box containing an unresolved quality hold.</p>

<h3>3.3 Mathematical Commitment Formulation</h3>
<p class="no-indent">Let <i>P</i> denote the public projection attributes and <i>S</i> denote internal enterprise metadata. The commitment <i>C</i> enqueued to the blockchain is defined as:</p>
<p class="no-indent" style="text-align: center; margin: 8pt 0;"><i>C = SHA-256( P || S || Nonce )</i></p>
<p class="no-indent">where <i>Nonce</i> is an off-chain cryptographically generated 128-bit random salt. The smart contract <font face="Courier New">WearTrailCommitmentStore.sol</font> registers <i>C</i> alongside the authoritative block timestamp, providing tamper evidence while mathematically concealing sensitive commercial details.</p>

<h2>4. Experimentation</h2>
<p>An experimental configuration was put up as a testbed to evaluate the latency, throughput, transactional consistency, and blockchain synchronization performance of WearTrail across all nine physical stations.</p>

