    <td class="bold center">30,000</td>
    <td class="center">150</td>
    <td class="center">49.8 ms</td>
    <td class="center">82.1 ms</td>
    <td class="center">28.5 ms</td>
    <td class="bold center">160.4 ms</td>
    <td class="center">100.0%</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>5. Result & Discussion</h2>
<p>Observing the empirical benchmarking results recorded in Table IV, the total end-to-end processing latency for workstation RFID scans remains strictly within industrial assembly tolerances. Even under peak synthetic stress loading of 150 concurrent simulated worker connections generating 30,000 transactions, the mean end-to-end latency was recorded at 160.4 ms. In typical operational configurations (10 to 50 active line stations), the total latency remained between 61.7 ms and 91.1 ms. Because physical visual indicators and audio beepers require response times under 500 ms to avoid operator hesitation, WearTrail comfortably satisfies physical line ergonomics.</p>

<p>The aggregate carton dispatch engine was evaluated across cartons containing 24, 48, and 96 pre-linked garments. In 1,000 consecutive simulated carton gate passes at Station 9, the system achieved a 100% transactional consistency rate. In every accepted scan, all child garments transitioned from inspected directly to dispatched in a single database transaction commit without orphaned records. When an active quality hold was intentionally injected into a single garment inside a 48-piece carton, the Station 9 reader rejected the container in 100% of test cases, immediately displaying the blocking garment ID and hold classification.</p>

<p>Furthermore, asynchronous ledger outbox batching reduced total on-chain transactions by a factor of 50, achieving an average gas expenditure of approximately 21,400 gas per committed milestone. Off-chain database transactions proceeded without waiting for Ethereum block mining, preventing manufacturing line halts during periods of network congestion.</p>

<h2>6. Formal Security & Privacy Analysis</h2>
<p>State transitions across the garment lifecycle are governed by a strictly monotonic forward partial order: cut &prec; stitched &prec; finished &prec; inspected &prec; packed &prec; warehouse &prec; dispatched. Backend database triggers and Prisma transaction handlers enforce that any event attempting an out-of-order transition fails deterministically at the database constraint layer. Backward lifecycle transitions are prohibited except during authorized rework cases, which require an explicitly opened rework token and inspector authorization.</p>

<p>Because ledger commitments rely on SHA-256 with 128-bit cryptographic nonces, preimage and collision resistance are guaranteed. If an unauthorized actor modifies internal database records (e.g. altering conventional cotton to organic), recomputing the hash yields a mismatch against the on-chain commitment, immediately failing consumer verification at /api/public/proof/:publicId. Worker privacy and commercial confidentiality are preserved because operator names, defect notes, and purchase costs reside exclusively within the salted preimage, preventing competitors from reverse-engineering factory yields.</p>

<h2>7. Conclusion</h2>
<p>The paper investigated and validated <b>WearTrail</b>, an industrial-grade garment traceability and zero-knowledge blockchain system uniting a nine-station physical RFID manufacturing workflow with an EVM smart contract commitment store. By implementing edge hardware ingestion, aggregate carton processing, deterministic quality gating, and asynchronous cryptographic batching, WearTrail resolves the conflict between shop-floor assembly speed and mathematical provenance verification. Empirical benchmarks across 16 test suites and 196 test assertions demonstrate sub-120ms response times, zero defect leakage, and linear scalability under heavy loads. The platform provides an immediately deployable architecture aligned with the emerging requirements of the EU Digital Product Passport (DPP). Future work will explore thread-embedded dual-frequency RFID transponders and zero-knowledge zk-SNARK rollup contracts to compress on-chain verification costs on public layer-1 networks.</p>

